(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Peristaltic movement of material through the GI tract is a function of which wall property?","choices":["Passive gravity flow","Ciliary beating action","Skeletal muscle only","Smooth muscle contraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Smooth muscle in the GI wall produces motility (peristalsis), which pushes material along the tract. — Slide 8 (General Structure)"},{"q":"The intrinsic innervation of the GI tract that coordinates smooth-muscle activity is the:","choices":["Phrenic nerve fibers","Sympathetic chain","Somatic motor system","Enteric nervous system"],"answer":3,"exp":"The enteric nervous system, distributed in two intramuscular plexuses (myenteric and submucosal), provides intrinsic innervation of the gut. — Slide 12 (Special Features)"},{"q":"The mesentery proper suspends which parts of the bowel?","choices":["The jejunum and ileum","The ascending and descending colon","The duodenum and pancreas","The rectum and anal canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mesentery proper suspends the jejunum and ileum and carries their vessels, lymphatics, and nerves. — Slide 22 (Mesentery)"},{"q":"The muscularis of the esophagus is unusual in that it contains:","choices":["Only cardiac muscle, unique to the heart","Only skeletal muscle fibers","Both skeletal and smooth muscle","Only elastic connective tissue fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The esophageal muscularis has inner circular and outer longitudinal layers containing both skeletal muscle (upper third) and smooth muscle (transitioning through the middle to entirely smooth in the lower third). — Slide 42 (Esophagus)"},{"q":"The longitudinal folds of the stomach lining that disappear as it fills are the:","choices":["Haustra","Plicae circulares","Rugae","Taeniae coli"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rugae are longitudinal gastric folds that flatten as the stomach fills; plicae circulares are permanent small-intestinal folds. — Slides 12, 58 (Stomach Interior)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the spleen is:","choices":["Intraperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Subperitoneal","Extraperitoneal"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spleen is intraperitoneal, lying anterior to ribs 9–11 and suspended by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"Compared with the ileum, the jejunum is characterized by:","choices":["A retroperitoneal position fixed to the wall","Thinner walls and prominent Peyer's patches","Larger diameter, thicker walls, and more vascularity","Absence of plicae circulares entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"The jejunum has a larger diameter, thicker walls, prominent plicae circulares, and richer vascularity than the ileum. — Slide 75 (Jejunum)"},{"q":"The narrowest-diameter, retroperitoneal segment continuous with the sigmoid colon is the:","choices":["Descending colon","Ascending colon","Transverse colon","Cecum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The descending colon is retroperitoneal, the narrowest in diameter, and continuous with the sigmoid colon. — Slide 96 (Descending Colon)"},{"q":"The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) drains into the duodenum at the:","choices":["Ampulla of Vater","Major papilla","Pyloric canal","Minor papilla"],"answer":3,"exp":"The accessory duct of Santorini joins the duodenum at the minor papilla. — Slide 74 (Duct of Santorini)"},{"q":"The bare area of the liver is significant because it is:","choices":["Covered by two layers of omentum","Where the gallbladder attaches","The site of the portal triad","Not covered by peritoneum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The liver is completely covered by Glisson's capsule but only incompletely by peritoneum, leaving a bare area surrounded by the peritoneal coronary ligaments. — Slides 104, 113 (Liver)"},{"q":"Within a liver lobule, blood ultimately collects into which vessel at the center?","choices":["The portal vein","The central vein","The bile duct","The hepatic artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Blood flows from the portal triad through the sinusoids to the central vein of each lobule. — Slide 121 (Histology of the liver)"},{"q":"The common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum and then through which structure before reaching the ampulla?","choices":["The transverse colon","The spleen","The left lobe of the liver","The head of the pancreas"],"answer":3,"exp":"The common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum, then through the head of the pancreas, terminating at the ampulla of Vater. — Slide 64 (Common Bile Duct)"},{"q":"The superior mesenteric artery primarily supplies which region of the gut?","choices":["The hindgut","The foregut","The midgut","The pelvic viscera"],"answer":2,"exp":"The SMA supplies midgut derivatives, from the distal duodenum through the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. — Slide 143 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"},{"q":"The IVC begins at approximately which vertebral level from the union of the common iliac veins?","choices":["T8","L5","T12","S1"],"answer":1,"exp":"The IVC begins around L5 where the common iliac veins unite, ascending to pierce the diaphragm at T8. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"The esophagus is clinically important for portosystemic anastomosis because it drains into the portal vein and the:","choices":["Splenic vein","Renal vein","Gonadal vein","Azygos vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Esophageal venous drainage reaches both the portal vein (via the left gastric vein) and the azygos system, making it a key portosystemic anastomosis site. — Slide 41 (Esophagus)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is also known by which name?","choices":["The infundibulum","The epiphysis","The hypophysis","The sella turcica"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pituitary gland is the hypophysis, attached to the hypothalamus by the infundibulum (pituitary stalk). — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"Which gland is subdivided into a cortex and a medulla that have entirely different embryonic origins and secretions?","choices":["Thyroid gland tissue","Suprarenal (adrenal) gland","Parathyroid gland tissue","Pineal gland tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each suprarenal (adrenal) gland has an outer cortex (steroid hormones, from mesoderm) and an inner medulla (catecholamines, from neural-crest-derived chromaffin cells) — two distinct embryonic origins. — Slides 5, 82 (Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex is composed of cells arranged in which pattern?","choices":["Ovoid clusters","Radial columns","Loose meshwork","Follicles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The zona glomerulosa has cells arranged in ovoid clusters and secretes mineralocorticoids (aldosterone). — Slide 87 (Histology of Adrenal Glands)"},{"q":"The zona reticularis is histologically arranged as a:","choices":["Simple cuboidal follicle","Radial column","Loose meshwork","Stratified sheet"],"answer":2,"exp":"The zona reticularis is arranged as a loose meshwork and secretes androgens. — Slide 87 (Histology of Adrenal Glands)"},{"q":"The thyroidea ima artery, an uncommon variant supplying the isthmus, arises from the arch of the aorta or the:","choices":["Internal carotid artery","Brachiocephalic artery","Subclavian artery","Facial artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thyroidea ima is a variant artery to the isthmus arising from the aortic arch or the brachiocephalic artery. — Slide 32 (Blood supply of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The superior and middle thyroid veins usually drain into which vessel?","choices":["Internal jugular vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Subclavian vein","External jugular vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular vein; the inferior thyroid vein drains to the brachiocephalic vein. — Slide 33 (Venous drainage)"},{"q":"Calcitriol raises blood calcium primarily by:","choices":["Stimulating calcitonin release","Increasing intestinal calcium absorption","Inhibiting the parathyroid glands","Filtering calcium in the kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"Calcitriol (active vitamin D) increases intestinal calcium absorption, raising blood calcium. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Kidney)"},{"q":"The gubernaculum testis contributes to descent because it:","choices":["Forms the tunica albuginea capsule of the testis itself","Actively contracts like a skeletal muscle fiber","Secretes testosterone to stimulate descent","Fails to elongate as the body grows, pulling the testis down"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gubernaculum does not elongate with body growth, so it draws the testes inferiorly toward the scrotum as the fetus grows around it. — Slide 9 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"The reproductive tract (ducts) functions to:","choices":["Produce gametes and hormones","Receive, store, and transport gametes","Secrete only alkaline lubricating mucus","Filter blood to remove metabolic waste"],"answer":1,"exp":"The reproductive tract consists of ducts that receive, store, and transport gametes. — Slide 4 (Organization of the Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The raphe of the scrotum is best described as:","choices":["A muscle elevating the testes reflexively","A thickened tissue ridge marking the scrotal septum","A serous membrane lining the scrotum","An arterial plexus supplying the scrotum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The raphe is a thickened tissue ridge (corresponding to the internal scrotal septum) extending from the anus across the scrotum. — Slide 16 (Scrotum)"},{"q":"Contraction of the dartos muscle produces which visible effect?","choices":["Erection of the penis","Elevation of the testes toward the abdomen","Wrinkling of the scrotal surface","Ejaculation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dartos contraction wrinkles the scrotal surface; cremaster contraction elevates the testes. — Slide 18 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"The pampiniform plexus drains blood from which structure?","choices":["The bladder","The prostate","The testis","The bulbo-urethral gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pampiniform plexus is the venous network draining the testis within the spermatic cord. — Slide 14 (Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"The pudendal nerve is listed among the nerves supplying which structure?","choices":["The kidney","The scrotum","The liver","The spleen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The scrotum receives supply from the hypogastric plexus, ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and pudendal nerves. — Slide 22 (scrotal nerve supply)"},{"q":"The seminal fluid from the seminal glands is rich in fructose, prostaglandins, and:","choices":["Clotting factors","Hyaluronidase","Seminalplasmin","Bicarbonate mucus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Seminal gland fluid contains fructose, prostaglandins, and clotting factors, which together nourish sperm and help coagulate semen after ejaculation. — Slide 55 (The Seminal Glands)"},{"q":"During emission, sperm are mixed with glandular secretions by peristalsis of the ductus deferens and which glands?","choices":["Adrenal glands sitting atop each kidney","Salivary glands of the digestive system","Seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands","Thyroid gland regulating whole-body metabolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Emission mixes sperm with secretions from the seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands via peristalsis of the ductus deferens. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"The spongy urethra ends at which structure?","choices":["The prostatic urethra","The internal urethral orifice","The ejaculatory duct","The external urethral orifice"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spongy (penile) urethra runs through the penis and ends at the external urethral orifice. — Slides 51, 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"In the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone, the Müllerian ducts develop into the:","choices":["Major internal female reproductive organs","Male reproductive tract via Wolffian ducts","Urinary bladder from the urogenital sinus","External genitalia only, not internal organs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Without anti-Müllerian hormone, the Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts persist and develop into the major internal female reproductive organs (uterine tubes, uterus, and upper vagina). — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts initially appear in:","choices":["Female embryos, exclusively","Both male and female embryos","Male embryos, exclusively","Neither until puberty"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Müllerian ducts are initially present in both male and female embryos, alongside the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The two perineal triangles share a base formed by a line between the:","choices":["Anterior superior iliac spines","Pubic tubercles","Ischial tuberosities","Ischial spines"],"answer":2,"exp":"An imaginary line between the ischial tuberosities forms the shared base dividing the anterior urogenital triangle from the posterior anal triangle. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The mons pubis is composed of skin and subcutaneous connective tissue overlying the:","choices":["Pubic symphysis","Coccyx","Ischial tuberosity","Sacral promontory"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mons pubis is an expanse of skin and subcutaneous fatty tissue anterior to the pubic symphysis. — Slides 8, 66 (Boundaries / External Genitalia)"},{"q":"The suspensory ligament extends from the mesovarium to the:","choices":["The uterine cervix","The uterine fundus","The labia minora fold","Body (pelvic/lateral) wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary extends from the mesovarium to the pelvic (lateral) body wall and carries the ovarian vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. — Slide 18 (Supporting Ligaments)"},{"q":"The pelvic diaphragm lifts to assist which function?","choices":["Release of feces during defecation","Filtration of urine within the nephron","Secretion of cervical mucus","Ovulation, triggered by an LH surge"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the pelvic organs and lifts to help release feces during defecation. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the ovaries occurs via a network termed the:","choices":["Prostatic plexus","Vesical venous plexus","Hypogastric plexus","Pampiniform plexus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ovarian veins drain via a pampiniform venous plexus, analogous to the male testicular pampiniform plexus. — Slide 27 (Vasculature of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The uterine tube receives its nerve supply from which plexi?","choices":["Celiac plexus only","Cardiac and pulmonary plexi","Ovarian and uterine plexi","Sacral plexus only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine tube's nerve supply comes from the ovarian and uterine (pelvic autonomic) plexi. — Slide 48 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The uterine tube region that passes through the uterine wall is the:","choices":["Infundibulum, the funnel-shaped ovarian end","Ampulla, the widest lateral segment","Intramural (interstitial) portion","Fimbriae, finger-like ovarian projections"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intramural (interstitial) portion of the uterine tube passes through the uterine wall to open into the uterine cavity. — Slide 51 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The uterine glands of the endometrium secrete glycoproteins that serve as:","choices":["Nutrients for a potential zygote","Digestive enzymes for the gastric lumen","Clotting factors made by the liver","Cervical mucus plug, a barrier secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Endometrial uterine glands secrete glycoproteins that serve as nutrients for a potential zygote before placental circulation is established. — Slide 43 (Endometrium)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the uterus is:","choices":["Extraperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Subperitoneal","Intraperitoneal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uterus is intraperitoneal, draped by the broad ligament, a double fold of peritoneum. — Slide 31 (Uterus)"},{"q":"The male urethra's first region, passing through the prostate, is the:","choices":["Prostatic urethra","Membranous urethra","Penile urethra","Spongy urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"The prostatic urethra is the first and widest portion of the male urethra, passing through the prostate gland. — Slide 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The penile (spongy) urethra ends at the:","choices":["External urethral orifice","Internal urethral orifice","Membranous urethra","Prostatic urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"The penile (spongy) urethra passes within the penile shaft and ends at the external urethral orifice, the opening at the tip of the glans. — Slide 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The internal space of the kidney that houses the renal pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat is the:","choices":["Renal cortex","Renal column","Renal papilla","Renal sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The renal sinus is the internal space housing the renal pelvis, calyces, vessels, nerves, and fat. — Slide 10 (Gross and Sectional Anatomy)"},{"q":"The fibrous capsule of the kidney directly covers which surface?","choices":["The medulla","The outer cortex","The renal pelvis","The minor calyces"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fibrous (renal) capsule, made of dense irregular connective tissue, covers the outer cortex of the kidney. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The peritubular capillaries are described as being:","choices":["Located only within the renal pelvis","Juxtaposed to the nephron for solute exchange","Part of the glomerular filtration membrane","Draining blood directly into the aorta"],"answer":1,"exp":"Peritubular capillaries are juxtaposed to the nephron tubules and, together with the vasa recta, perform solute exchange with the filtrate. — Slide 18 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Juxtamedullary nephrons are essential for which function?","choices":["Concentrating urine via long loops and vasa recta","Producing renin exclusively for all nephrons","Storing urine within the collecting ducts","Providing voluntary control of urination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons, with their long loops of Henle and accompanying vasa recta, are essential for concentrating urine by recovering water and solutes deep in the medulla. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"The ureter is clinically noted to travel deep (inferior) to which vessels bilaterally?","choices":["The inferior mesenteric artery","The renal vessels at the kidney hilum","The common iliac artery near the pelvic brim","The gonadal (testicular/ovarian) vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"The 'water under the bridge' relationship: the ureter passes deep (posterior/inferior) to the gonadal vessels bilaterally. — Slide 48 ('Water under the bridge')"},{"q":"The ureter mucosa is lined by transitional epithelium, which provides:","choices":["Enzyme secretion only","Stretch (distensibility)","Ciliary motion only","Filtration of the passing urine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Transitional epithelium lines the ureters (and bladder), allowing them to stretch and change shape as urine passes through and accumulates. — Slide 45 (Ureter Walls)"}]};
